<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false">
        <!--
            `allowBackup` was `true`, which is the template default and the wrong
            answer for this application. Android Auto Backup copies an
            application's private data directory to the person's Google Drive
            and restores it onto whatever device they sign in to next. This
            shell's data directory holds the WebView cookie store, and that
            store holds the Supabase session cookie the middleware refreshes on
            every navigation, so leaving backup on means an authenticated
            Vallo session, with a wallet behind it, travelling through a cloud
            backup and landing on a second handset. There is nothing in here
            worth restoring: the account lives on the server and signing in
            again takes seconds.

            `dataExtractionRules` closes the half `allowBackup` does not. From
            Android 12, for an application targeting 31 or above,
            `allowBackup="false"` stops CLOUD backup but NOT device-to-device
            transfer (the cable or wifi migration to a new phone). The rules
            file excludes every domain from both, so the cookie jar and its
            session never travel either way.

            `usesCleartextTraffic` is already false by default at this target
            SDK, and it is written out anyway because it is the flag that
            silently flips back if anyone ever lowers the target. The web
            platform sends HSTS with preload for the same reason, and
            `capacitor.config.ts` sets `cleartext: false` on the server block.
            All three have to agree or the weakest one wins.
        -->

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
                Android App Links. This is the half of the deep link contract
                that lives in the binary; the other half is
                `apps/web/public/.well-known/assetlinks.json`, and NEITHER WORKS
                WITHOUT THE OTHER.

                `android:autoVerify="true"` makes Android fetch
                `https://<host>/.well-known/assetlinks.json` at install time and
                compare the signing certificate fingerprint in that file against
                the one the installed binary was actually signed with. If they
                match, every listed URL opens here instead of in a browser,
                with no disambiguation dialogue and no way for another
                application to claim our links. If they do not match, or the
                file is missing, verification fails quietly and every link falls
                back to the browser. That failure is invisible unless somebody
                goes looking, so verify it deliberately after the first release:

                    adb shell pm get-app-links com.vallospaces.app

                and read the state beside each host. Anything other than
                `verified` almost always means the fingerprint in
                `assetlinks.json` is wrong, so here is where the owner gets the
                two values that file is waiting for. BOTH belong in the array,
                and leaving either one out is the failure people spend a day on.

                First, the Play app signing key. Play Console, the app, Release,
                Setup, App signing, then the field labelled "SHA-256 certificate
                fingerprint" under App signing key certificate. THIS IS THE ONE
                THAT MATTERS on a shipped install, because Play App Signing
                strips our upload signature and re-signs the binary with a key
                Google holds, so the certificate on the device is never the one
                we signed with.

                Second, the upload key. That one is ours, it is the fingerprint
                of the keystore named in `android/keystore.properties`, and it
                is read locally with

                    keytool -list -v -keystore upload-keystore.jks -alias upload

                taking the line beginning SHA256. It is in the array because
                every build installed by hand, from Android Studio or a direct
                APK, carries the upload signature rather than Google's, and
                without it App Links never verify during testing.

                Both are 32 colon-separated hex pairs in upper case. The
                placeholders in `assetlinks.json` are deliberately not hex, so
                a file that was never filled in fails verification loudly rather
                than looking plausible.

                THE HOST IS `www.vallospaces.com` ALONE (native release audit,
                28 September 2026). It is the canonical host: the live site's
                canonical and og:url are www, and every link the product prints
                is built from the request's own host, which is www.

                THE APEX, AND WHY IT IS NOT CLAIMED. `https://vallospaces.com/`
                answers EVERY path, `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` included,
                with a 308 to www. That redirect is Vercel's domain setting,
                not repository code. Android's verifier does not follow
                redirects, so the apex can never verify while it stands, and
                on Android 11 and below verification is ALL OR NOTHING: one
                host that fails means NO host is verified, so claiming the apex
                would have cost www its App Links on exactly the older handsets
                this product is built for. To bring the apex back, the owner
                must first make the apex serve `/.well-known/*` with a 200 and
                no redirect, then add the host here, in `App.entitlements`,
                and in `src/lib/brand-domain.test.ts`.

                The path prefixes are the shareable half of the product and
                nothing else. A listing forwarded on WhatsApp is the case that
                pays for this whole feature, and the others are the addresses
                the product itself tells people to share, PLUS ONE RETURN PATH
                that had to be added and is the reason Google sign-in could not
                complete on the native shell at all.

                `/auth/callback` IS IN, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY THE
                OPPOSITE. It said `/auth/` was deliberately absent because OAuth
                "completes in the system browser where their cookies and PKCE
                verifier live". That is false for this codebase and the file it
                contradicted was right: `startOAuth` runs as a SERVER ACTION
                from the web view (`src/lib/auth/actions.ts`), so Supabase's
                PKCE verifier cookie is set on the WEB VIEW's cookie jar. If
                the in-app tab loads the callback, the exchange runs in a jar
                with no verifier, the single-use code is spent, and the person
                lands back in an application that is still signed out. See
                `src/lib/native/deep-links.ts`, which says this in its own
                words, and the matching include in
                `public/.well-known/apple-app-site-association`.

                STILL deliberately absent: `/checkout/` and `/wallet`. Those
                two are Paystack returns and they are settled by the
                server-to-server webhook rather than by the return trip, so the
                money is already recorded and the journey back is cosmetic. The
                REST of `/auth/` is absent too: a sign-in page, a password reset
                form and an email confirmation are pages a person should meet
                wherever they opened them.

                WHY EACH PREFIX IS IN OR OUT, because this list was wrong once
                and the way it was wrong was invisible. Until R3's F-02 this
                filter listed the Property side and the social layer and NOT ONE
                Stays path, so every hotel, shortlet and restaurant link fell
                through to a browser. The side law in
                `src/lib/side.constants.ts` forces the Stays shell for
                `/stay`, `/stays`, `/restaurant` and `/restaurants`, and it is
                correct; it simply never ran, because the operating system had
                already decided the URL was not ours. A product whose signature
                interaction is the flip between two sides cannot have one of
                those sides unreachable from a shared link.

                IN, because somebody forwards them: `/listing/` and `/stay/`
                (the two detail pages, one per side), `/restaurant/`,
                `/stays/` and `/restaurants/` (the shelves and their searches,
                which is how a group decides where to stay), `/around/`, `/u/`,
                `/post/` and `/stories/` (the social addresses the product
                prints to people as their own).

                OUT, and each for a stated reason rather than by omission:
                `/trips` and `/bookings` are signed-in own-data surfaces, so a
                forwarded link can only ever land its reader on a sign-in wall
                inside the app, which is a worse first meeting than a browser
                tab; `/home` and `/search` are the Property roots, which the
                app opens on anyway; `/checkout/`, `/wallet` and `/auth/` are
                the Paystack return paths named above, and everything under
                `/auth/` except the callback.

                The bare roots `/stays` and `/restaurants` without a trailing
                slash are not listed, matching `/home` and `/search`: a shelf
                root is not an address anybody forwards, and `pathPrefix` has
                no word boundary, so `/stays` would also claim any future path
                that merely begins with those letters.

                Worth knowing before this is switched on: `/listing/`, `/u/`,
                `/post/`, `/stories/` and `/around/` are all protected product
                segments in `src/middleware.ts`. A signed-out person following a
                shared link arrives at the sign-in redirect rather than at the
                listing, inside the application exactly as on the web. That is
                existing product behaviour, not something this filter causes,
                but it is what the owner will see the first time they test it.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <!-- www ONLY, and the apex is out on purpose: see "THE APEX"
                     in the note above this filter. -->
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="www.vallospaces.com" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/listing/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/stay/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/stays/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/restaurant/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/restaurants/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/around/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/u/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/post/" />
                <data android:pathPrefix="/stories/" />
                <!-- The auth return. Social sign-in is off inside the shell
                     (STORE-02/03), but email confirmation and reset links for
                     an account made in the app still need to come back to the
                     web view that holds the PKCE verifier. See the note above. -->
                <data android:pathPrefix="/auth/callback" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS" android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

        <!--
            FCM's defaults for a notification that arrives while the
            application is not running.

            WHY THE CHANNEL ID MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. From Android 8
            every notification must belong to a channel, and a notification
            naming a channel that does not exist IS DROPPED WITHOUT A SOUND
            OR A LOG LINE. `vallo_default` is the id the server puts on every
            Android message (`src/lib/push/transport/fcm.ts`), so the two have
            to agree; if this ever changes, that file changes with it. The
            channel itself is created at boot by `src/lib/native/push-taps.ts`.

            THESE THREE MUST BE DIRECT CHILDREN OF <application>. Until 28
            September 2026 they sat inside the FileProvider's <provider>
            element, where they were provider metadata that Firebase never
            reads (it reads `ApplicationInfo.metaData`). The file was well
            formed, so nothing complained. `src/lib/native/native-projects.test.ts`
            now holds the placement.

            The icon must be a silhouette: Android 5 and above renders a
            notification icon as a mask, so anything with colour in it comes
            out a white blob. `@mipmap/ic_launcher` is wrong for the same
            reason and is the default people fall back to.

            NONE OF THIS DOES ANYTHING WITHOUT A VALID `app/google-services.json`.
            The file is in the repository for Firebase project `vallo-44059`
            but its `current_key` is still a placeholder, so `app/build.gradle`
            refuses a release build until the owner pastes the real Android
            API key in.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_channel_id"
            android:value="vallo_default" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_icon"
            android:resource="@drawable/ic_stat_vallo" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_color"
            android:resource="@color/vallo_notification" />
    </application>

    <!--
        PERMISSIONS.

        The inventory in `docs/MOBILE_READINESS.md` section 4 is the source of
        truth for this list, and it was compiled from the code rather than from
        habit. The rule it sets is that the native build declares exactly what
        the web build already declares and nothing more, because the edge sends

            Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), geolocation=(self)

        on every response, and a native shell that asks for more than the site
        it loads is asking for something the site cannot use. (Until VC1, 8
        October 2026, the edge sent `camera=(), microphone=()`; voice and video
        calls are why both are now `(self)`, and why CAMERA, RECORD_AUDIO and
        MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS are declared below.)
    -->

    <!--
        Required. The shell has no packaged application to run: it loads the
        live origin over https, so with no network there is nothing but the
        offline card in `native-shell`.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Location. COARSE is what the product needs, and the FINE line below it
        is not a contradiction. Read both comments before changing either.

        WHAT THE PRODUCT NEEDS, CORRECTED. This paragraph used to say there was
        "exactly one consumer of location on this platform" and that "nothing
        stores the position: it is read in the browser and never posted". Both
        were false, and a false statement in this file is worse than a false
        statement almost anywhere else, because the paragraph at the bottom
        told whoever fills Play's Data safety form what to answer.

        THE TWO CONSUMERS, WHICH IS NOW THE WHOLE LIST:

          1. The locate-me control in
             `src/components/app/search/MapCanvas.tsx:489`. All it does with the
             fix is `setView([lat, lng], 13)`. Zoom 13 shows several kilometres
             of a city across a 390px screen, so a coarse fix of one to three
             kilometres lands inside the same view a precise one would have
             produced. Nothing is posted. This one always matched the old
             description.

          2. The drop-the-pin control in
             `src/components/host/HostWizard.tsx:616`. A host standing in the
             property they are listing taps it, and the coordinate is rounded
             to six decimals and SAVED THROUGH `addAccommodationDraft` AS THE
             LATITUDE AND LONGITUDE OF THAT PROPERTY. It leaves the device, it
             is stored, and it is published on the listing as the property's
             address. That is the whole point of the control, and it is a fact
             about a property rather than a trail behind a person, but it is
             still location that leaves the device and the form has to say so.

        A third consumer, `TravelTime`, posted a precise fix to
        `/api/travel-time`, a route that has never existed in this tree. It has
        been deleted rather than described.

        So coarse is still the honest ask: consumer 1 does not need precision,
        and consumer 2 is a host deliberately marking a building they are
        standing in, where an approximate fix plus the address they type is the
        real answer and the coordinate is a convenience. Both controls pass
        `enableHighAccuracy: true`, but that is only ever a hint, and on
        Android 12 and above this application cannot request precise location
        at all, because of the `maxSdkVersion` cap below.

        `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` is absent and must stay absent. Nothing
        runs when the application is not in front of the person.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
        AND WHY FINE IS HERE ANYWAY, CAPPED AT ANDROID 11.

        This is not a hedge, it is a defect in the layer underneath us, and
        removing this line breaks the locate control on every device below
        Android 12. The reasoning, from
        `@capacitor/android` 8.5.0, `BridgeWebChromeClient.java`:

        When the web layer asks for a position, the bridge intercepts it in
        `onGeolocationPermissionsShowPrompt` and requests BOTH coarse and fine
        as one batch. Its result handler then ANDs the outcomes, so a batch in
        which fine came back denied is treated as an outright refusal. There is
        a rescue path for exactly this case, but it is guarded on
        `Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= S`: on Android 12 and above, a granted coarse
        permission is accepted on its own and the web callback fires.

        Below Android 12 there is no such path. An undeclared permission is
        denied by the system without a prompt, so with coarse alone the batch
        can never come back all-true, and the bridge tells the web layer the
        person refused. The locate control would report "denied" and degrade
        into its designed state, which means the failure is quiet and correct
        looking, and nobody would connect it to this file. Android 11 and below
        is not a rounding error on the handsets this product is built for.

        `android:maxSdkVersion="30"` is what makes this an honest declaration
        rather than a widened one. Android 12 is where approximate location
        became a first-class choice a person can make in the prompt, and it is
        also exactly where the bridge learned to accept it. So fine location is
        held only on the versions that have no way to express what we actually
        want, and it is stripped from the merged manifest everywhere else. On a
        modern handset this application cannot request precise location at all.

        THE DATA SAFETY INSTRUCTION THAT USED TO BE HERE WAS WRONG AND
        FOLLOWING IT WOULD HAVE BEEN A FALSE DECLARATION TO GOOGLE. It read:
        "approximate location, used for app functionality, not shared, not
        stored". "Not stored" is false, because of consumer 2 above.

        DO NOT ANSWER THE FORM FROM THIS FILE. The row by row answers, derived
        from what the code actually does rather than from a comment, are in
        `docs/research/STORE_REJECTION_RISK_RESEARCH.md` Part D.5, and they are
        the only place that answer is maintained. The one thing worth repeating
        here, because it is the thing this file got wrong: LOCATION IS
        COLLECTED AND IT IS STORED. Approximate location, collected, stored,
        used for app functionality, and published on a listing when a host uses
        the drop-the-pin control. Precise location is not collected on Android
        12 and above and cannot be, because of the cap above.

        None of the bridge reasoning above has been on a handset. It is read
        from the bridge source in `node_modules/@capacitor/android`, and it is
        the kind of claim that deserves a real device before the first release.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" android:maxSdkVersion="30" />

    <!--
        POST_NOTIFICATIONS, the runtime permission for showing a notification
        at all, added when push became a channel that can deliver. See the
        long note further down for what changed and what has not.

        IT ONLY EXISTS ON ANDROID 13, API 33, AND ABOVE. Below that the
        permission is granted at install and there is no prompt, which is why
        `components/app/push/moments.ts` skips the explanation screen entirely
        on older handsets rather than asking somebody to agree to something
        that has already happened. Declaring it here is harmless on those
        versions and required on newer ones.

        A PERMANENT DENIAL IS NOT A PROMPT TO REPEAT. Android stops showing
        the system dialogue after two refusals and returns denied without
        asking. The route back is the application's own notification settings
        in the system Settings app, and the product says so rather than
        calling the request again into a dialogue that will never appear.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        VC1 (8 October 2026): VOICE AND VIDEO CALLS. CAMERA, RECORD_AUDIO AND
        MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS ARE NOW DECLARED, and this is why each is needed.

        A call screen asks for a camera and microphone stream with
        `getUserMedia` inside the web view. The WebView turns that into
        `WebChromeClient.onPermissionRequest`, and Capacitor's own
        `BridgeWebChromeClient` (8.5.2, read from node_modules) already
        handles it: VIDEO_CAPTURE requests CAMERA, AUDIO_CAPTURE requests
        MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS and RECORD_AUDIO, as one runtime batch, and
        grants the page only if all come back granted. So MainActivity needs
        no WebChromeClient of its own. What it needs is the declarations: an
        undeclared permission is refused by the system WITHOUT a prompt, the
        batch fails, and the call screen sees "permission denied" with no
        dialogue ever shown. MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS is a normal (install time)
        permission, never a prompt, but it is in the same batch, so it must be
        declared too or every audio request fails.

        The page that asks is only ever our own origin: `server.url` is the
        www host and `allowNavigation` lists only our hosts, and the site's
        `Permissions-Policy` allows the camera and microphone for `self` alone.

        THE SIDE EFFECT ON PHOTO CAPTURE, recorded so nobody is surprised.
        Before VC1 the paragraph below kept CAMERA undeclared so that a
        `capture` file input went straight to the system camera app with no
        grant. With CAMERA declared, `BridgeWebChromeClient.isMediaCaptureSupported()`
        now asks for the permission first (once; a person who allowed it for a
        call is not asked again), and the Camera plugin does the same. A refusal
        returns no photo to that input, and the person can still choose a file
        from the gallery. That trade is accepted for calls.

        The three `uses-feature` lines keep the app installable on a device
        without a camera or microphone: declaring CAMERA otherwise IMPLIES
        `android.hardware.camera` as required, and Play would hide the app
        from those devices. Such a device gets voice calls, or a clear
        "no camera found" on the call screen.

        Background ringing, ConnectionService and a lock-screen call UI are
        NOT built: no FOREGROUND_SERVICE, no MANAGE_OWN_CALLS, no
        USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT. See docs/video-calling/VIDEO-CALLING-MOBILE-COMPATIBILITY.md.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.microphone" android:required="false" />

    <!--
        Not declared, on purpose, so that the next person does not add them
        back wondering whether they were forgotten:

        CAMERA, AND THIS PARAGRAPH IS NOW HISTORY: VC1 DECLARES IT ABOVE, for
        calls. What follows is kept as the record of the earlier reasoning.
        Listing photos, avatars, verification documents and message
        attachments all arrive through an ordinary `<input type="file">`. On
        Android that opens the system document picker, which reads the file on
        the application's behalf and needs no grant of ours.

        This one is worth stating precisely rather than by habit, because
        Capacitor's `onShowFileChooser` DOES request the camera permission, and
        the condition it does it under is `fileChooserParams.isCaptureEnabled()`
        together with an `image/*` accept. That flag comes from the `capture` attribute on the input.

        CORRECTED 28 SEPTEMBER 2026: this used to say no `capture` attribute
        existed. Two do (`ShowMePanel` for video, `ArrivalCheckCard` for
        photos), and the listing wizard calls the Camera plugin. None of them
        needs this permission, and that is the reason it stays undeclared:
        `BridgeWebChromeClient.isMediaCaptureSupported()` returns true when
        CAMERA is NOT declared, and the bridge then hands the capture to the
        system camera app by intent, which needs no grant of ours. Declaring
        CAMERA would flip that branch into a runtime prompt, and a refusal
        would return nothing to the input. The Camera plugin's own manifest
        declares no permission either.

        READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE and READ_MEDIA_IMAGES. Same reason, and this is
        the one people get wrong. The Storage Access Framework hands back a
        content URI the application already has read rights to. Declaring a
        media permission here would trigger a Play policy declaration for
        broad file access that this application cannot justify, and it would
        gain nothing.

        READ_CONTACTS, READ_CALENDAR. Nothing in the product reads an address
        book or writes a calendar entry. (RECORD_AUDIO used to be on this line;
        VC1 declares it above, for calls. Calls are not recorded.)

        POST_NOTIFICATIONS USED TO BE ON THIS LIST AND IS NOT ANY MORE.
        It is declared above, and this paragraph is the record of why the
        reasoning changed rather than a quiet deletion of it.

        What it said was: "Web Push is not wired and there is no FCM
        registration, so asking for the notification prompt today would spend
        the one prompt a person reliably grants on a channel that cannot yet
        deliver anything." That was correct and it was the right call for as
        long as it was true. A manifest whose comments say the opposite of its
        declarations is how the next reader is misled, so it moves when the
        fact moves.

        WHAT CHANGED. `supabase/migrations/*_push_one_*` and `*_push_two_*`
        built the token table and the delivery path, `src/lib/push/` carries
        three transports, and the permission is now asked for by
        `src/components/app/push/PushPrompt.tsx` AFTER a Vallo screen a person
        has already said yes to, at one of four moments where the value is
        obvious. The prompt is no longer spent on a cold start, which was the
        real objection in the sentence above.

        WHAT HAS STILL NOT CHANGED, AND IT MATTERS ON THIS PLATFORM
        SPECIFICALLY. Android delivery needs FCM, FCM needs a service account
        AND `app/google-services.json`, and neither exists in this repository
        because both come from a Firebase project only the owner can create.
        Until they do, an Android handset grants the permission and receives
        nothing. `src/lib/push/credentials.ts` names the missing variables and
        who supplies them, and the drain refuses to claim work rather than
        failing quietly against a wall.
    -->

    <!--
        Package visibility, not a permission.

        From Android 11 an application cannot see which other applications can
        handle an intent unless it says what it is looking for. Without this
        block `@capacitor/browser` cannot resolve a Custom Tabs provider, and
        the external handoff described in `capacitor.config.ts` is exactly
        where that matters: a Paystack checkout and an OAuth sign-in have to
        open in the system browser rather than inside our WebView, and a failed
        resolution there is a dead payment button rather than a visible error.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
